{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/global-palette-substitution.md", "content": "# Global palette substitution in noisy grids

A task may present a random-looking field with only two colors and no meaningful geometry. Test cellwise global color substitution as an early candidate: compare input color roles and exact output values across complete demo grids. Strong evidence is that every occurrence of a given input color maps to one output color, and the alternate input color maps to another, with no coordinate dependence. Demos can use distinct palettes, dimensions, and marker densities; compare the mappings rather than assuming literal colors transfer between examples.

Once validated, apply the mapping to every test cell without changing dimensions. Check all rows and columns mechanically. Avoid inferring a mapping from just one demo if the two colors' roles or mappings vary by example.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/global-palette-substitution.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/palette-and-operation-validation.md", "content": "# Palette and operation validation across demos

A candidate grid must be reconciled against every full demonstration before submission. Track transformation extent/geometry separately from input-to-output color mapping, then test their combined predictions. A binary rejection falsifies the compound rule; it does not identify whether geometry, extent, or palette was wrong. Do not merely swap output colors or preserve rejected geometry unless the demos independently establish that change.

When demos vary in dimensions and palettes, do not transfer literal output colors by intuition or treat examples as unrelated. Establish whether cellwise color changes are global and role-based, and seek one common mechanism that predicts every pair. Use exact changed/unchanged cells as constraints; discard no inconvenient example. Submit only after checking the predicted output against those constraints.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/palette-and-operation-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/post-rejection-extent-color-audit.md", "content": "# Post-rejection extent and color audit

A binary wrong result rejects the complete proposed rule, not just a guessed cell. Before another request or submission, revisit all demos and partition the hypothesis into (1) trigger, (2) selected object or stroke extent, and (3) replacement color. Record exactly which cells change and which similar motifs remain untouched. Do not let a familiar visual analogy override these constraints.

For thin strokes among larger color masses, test the relation of each candidate stroke to mass boundaries and endpoints. A stroke color may be replaced by a mass color without changing the mass itself, while visually similar strokes can remain unchanged if the trigger is more specific. Verify each proposed trigger and transformed cell against every demo. A wrong first candidate is reason to rebuild the mapping, not to repeat the same theory or request another example without a concrete discriminating question.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/post-rejection-extent-color-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/marker-ray-continuation.md", "content": "# Extending marker patterns as rays

When sparse input markers appear to encode a geometric trace, use demonstrations to infer its direction, step size, and colors rather than adding a one-cell continuation by intuition. Compare input and output coordinates: identify the seed points, infer the repeated displacement vector(s), and check the output for each predicted point. If the demo shows two rays or parallel traces, model both. Apply only the demonstrated continuation, stopping at the grid boundary and leaving unrelated cells unchanged.

A failed submission rejects the complete hypothesis but does not identify the bad cell. Revisit the full demo evidence and distinguish structural alternatives (for example, a single adjacent extension versus a repeated diagonal ray) before spending another attempt or requesting more examples.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/marker-ray-continuation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/multiple-outline-effects.md", "content": "# Distinguishing multiple outlined-object effects

When a scene contains several hollow or outlined motifs, create a per-object ledger: bounds, border color, interior pattern, embedded exceptional cells, and exact output changes. Infer the trigger independently from the operation and its extent. A task may combine independent effects—for example, filling the interior of one enclosure while changing an exceptional-color center cell in other motifs. Do not assume that every similar-looking enclosure receives the same operation or that all interiors should be filled globally.

Compare each object role across every demo, including unchanged objects and preserved borders. After a rejected output, discard the whole selection/operation hypothesis and audit every object rather than tweaking isolated cells. Request a demo only if competing object-selection rules predict different test outputs and the information justifies its cost.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/multiple-outline-effects.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/object-normalization-after-rejection.md", "content": "# Object normalization after a rejected grid

When an input contains regular colored regions interrupted by sparse off-color pixels, don't automatically preserve those pixels or treat every anomaly as an independent trigger. Compare each complete object in every demo with its output: outputs may normalize an entire object to its dominant color while removing embedded intrusions, or may remove isolated markers without altering the shape. Separate object selection from cell treatment, and check regions that look similar but remain unchanged.

After binary rejection, the whole candidate transformation is disproven but the feedback gives no localization. Reconstruct the object map and changed/unchanged cells from all demos; avoid local edits to a speculative test grid. Only submit again after a revised rule explains all demonstrations.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/object-normalization-after-rejection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Reconcile heterogeneous or seemingly contradictory demos

With costly demos that vary in size, palette, and visual structure, compare each input/output pair at the exact changed-cell level before choosing an action. Record preserved cells/regions and unchanged pairs as constraints. Normalize semantic color roles only where the edits support the mapping; superficial resemblance to a known task or a remembered reference is not validation.

Test a proposed shared rule against every example, separating trigger, selected extent, and replacement mapping. If some pairs appear incompatible, state that uncertainty instead of ignoring inconvenient examples. After a rejected submission, reject the whole compound hypothesis and rebuild the evidence ledger; do not switch transformation families based solely on unrelated skill notes or make unsupported local edits. Spend another demo only when explicit surviving alternatives predict materially different test outputs and the information justifies its cost. Maintain the required single-action JSON protocol.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/panel-exception-lesson.md", "content": "# Panel classification: exceptions and post-rejection audit

In separator-panel tasks, correct panel dimensions do not validate a marker-presence label rule. Across every demonstration, make a full table of panels and outputs, recording marker presence, count, and arrangement. A marked panel may share the background output label while another marked panel receives the special label; train the signature against all examples rather than extrapolating from early uniformly labeled demos.

For a rejected output, treat the whole mapping as suspect. Recheck panel extraction and reading order, then revisit what feature distinguishes labels. Do not merely edit a few cells based on binary feedback. Request another example only if surviving signatures predict different useful outcomes and the cost is justified; otherwise apply the strongest candidate consistently to all test panels.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/panel-exception-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/noisy-background-pattern-propagation.md", "content": "# Noisy-background pattern propagation checkpoint

When a small colored motif is embedded in a mostly two-color noisy field and outputs add that motif color at many scattered locations, do not infer a rule from visual resemblance alone. Compare every input-output pair cell by cell and form the exact changed-cell mask. Test exact motif placements, orientations, overlaps, and near-matches; conspicuous uncolored near-matches are negative evidence that a candidate selection rule must explain. Check that original marker cells are preserved and that all additions—not merely many of them—follow one consistent criterion. Before requesting another demo, state at least two surviving rules, their distinct predictions for the active test, and the concrete example outcome that would discriminate them. A further noisy grid without such a discriminator is not an evidence-led request.

This is a general audit method, not a claim that motif-copying is the right explanation for every noisy grid. See also `noisy-region-hypothesis-audit.md` and `overlapping-pattern-copy-audit.md` for connectivity and false-positive placement pitfalls.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/noisy-background-pattern-propagation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/unchanged-demo-and-failed-hypothesis.md", "content": "# Unchanged demos and hypothesis revision

An input-output pair with no changes is an active constraint, not a non-example. A salient sparse motif may be inert or lack the feature that triggers the demonstrated operation. Compare every demo for changed and preserved coordinates, including clusters that look similar but receive different treatment.

After a wrong submission, reject the complete hypothesis and revisit the transformation family; don't make cosmetic coordinate edits. Separate trigger, propagation geometry, selected extent, and handling of original markers. A single demo that extends a diagonal may support a ray hypothesis, but unchanged sparse clusters elsewhere are counterevidence to blindly extending every similar-looking track. Request more only when an example can distinguish specific alternatives; otherwise form a materially revised candidate from the full evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/unchanged-demo-and-failed-hypothesis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/rejected-fill-candidate-review.md", "content": "# Reassessing a rejected broad transformation

A binary incorrect submission rejects the entire candidate rule; it does not localize the error to a particular cell or boundary. Re-derive the rule using the demos rather than making a small cosmetic change to the submitted grid. Explicitly inspect the proposed trigger, transformed extent, and conspicuous regions left untouched by demonstrations. For region-fill hypotheses, distinguish changing the target object's cells from recoloring surrounding background, deleting markers, or modifying unrelated shapes. If these interpretations predict different test outputs, request a demo only when its expected information is worth the fixed cost; otherwise submit a materially revised, evidence-grounded candidate.

During strict evaluator turns, emit exactly one protocol-valid JSON action and no commentary.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/rejected-fill-candidate-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/repeated-noisy-task-failure.md", "content": "# Repeated failure and evidence discipline

In a costly continual-ARC task, many demonstrations and repeated binary rejections can still fail to identify the rule if the examples are not explicitly reconciled. Before the next action after each pair, record what changed and what stayed fixed across all demonstrations, separating trigger, transformed extent, direction, color mapping, and exceptions. Compare normalized geometry across demos even when grid sizes and palettes differ.

A wrong submission rejects the whole candidate transformation; it does not identify a faulty cell. After rejection, materially revise the structural hypothesis and validate its predictions against every demo. Do not cycle through unmodified-input answers, arbitrary single-cell changes, or unsupported remembered patterns. When demos are exhausted, use remaining attempts for distinct hypotheses that explain the evidence, rather than cosmetic variants.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/repeated-noisy-task-failure.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/inert-demo-structures.md", "content": "# Preserved motifs are evidence too

When a demo's output is identical to its input, treat that as a real constraint rather than a non-example. A salient colored cluster can be structurally irrelevant, a distractor, or insufficiently contextualized to trigger the task rule. Compare across demos for motifs that change versus motifs that remain untouched, then apply only the supported transformation to the test. Avoid transforming a test cluster merely because it resembles the changed motif in shape or colors; establish which feature actually distinguishes operative from inert structures. If demonstrations do not resolve that distinction, request a targeted demo only when its expected information is worth the cost.
