{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/rejected-attempt-ledger-discipline.md", "content": "# After repeated binary rejection: rebuild, don't permute

A binary-wrong grid does not reveal which cell or subrule failed. Do not send a cosmetically altered copy of the previous output. Reconcile all demonstrations afresh in a compact ledger: exact changed-cell masks, salient unchanged near-matches, marker/object geometry and role, plus each candidate's trigger, extent, and color treatment. For every candidate, mechanically compare predicted changes against every pair, including unchanged counterexamples. A candidate that has not passed this audit is not a materially revised hypothesis. Avoid substituting repeated skill/reference lookups for evidence work. If the demo budget is exhausted, choose the strongest rule supported by that ledger; never represent an unverified guess as validated.

Keep the evaluator response to exactly one protocol-valid JSON action.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/rejected-attempt-ledger-discipline.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/rejected-grid-rule-audit.md", "content": "# Rule audit after a rejected submission

A wrong submission rejects the whole hypothesis, not just one guessed cell. Re-derive from all demo pairs rather than iterating local edits. In cluttered inputs, identify regular object geometry and compare each output cell: isolated cells of other colors within otherwise uniform shapes may be noise or overlays, and the output may normalize the whole object while removing those intrusions. Conversely, do not generalize this without demo support. Audit both the transformed extent and every unusual cell outside it; test the candidate against every demo before spending another attempt.

